WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.782
A single customer reply can require several calls to a model.

00:00:04.782 --> 00:00:08.031
A call sends text in and receives text back.

00:00:08.031 --> 00:00:12.121
The service measures that text in pieces called tokens.

00:00:12.121 --> 00:00:16.229
The total cost of an answer adds up across the calls.

00:00:16.229 --> 00:00:18.730
Latency is waiting time.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:23.213
It includes time spent waiting for tools as well as the model.

00:00:23.213 --> 00:00:30.233
Seeing the first words sooner and finishing the whole job sooner are related but different improvements.

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:36.571
Monitoring means recording enough about each request to spot changes across the day.

00:00:36.571 --> 00:00:44.240
A sudden rise in waiting time means more when you can also see whether a particular lookup started failing.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.847
Ana asks Orbit where her tent is.

00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:52.045
Scout first looks up the order, then checks the carrier, then writes a reply.

00:00:52.045 --> 00:00:57.329
Read the example receipt as three charges for one customer answer.

00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:04.774
The first charge is one point four cents, the next is one point six, and the last is two point one.

00:01:04.774 --> 00:01:11.333
Add them together and the answer costs five point one cents at these example rates.

00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:20.713
This illustrative bill belongs to Ana's order enquiry. Read it as an example of adding charges, rather than a price list.

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:28.395
Step one sends three thousand tokens and gets back a short call to get order: about one and a half cents.

00:01:28.395 --> 00:01:35.406
Step two sends the whole conversation again, now with the order details, and calls track parcel.

00:01:35.406 --> 00:01:41.614
Step three sends everything once more and writes the answer, which costs about two cents.

00:01:41.614 --> 00:01:49.604
In total, ten and a half thousand input tokens and four hundred and fifty output tokens: about five cents.

00:01:49.604 --> 00:01:57.710
At four hundred tickets a day, that is about twenty dollars a day, far less than the hours Leo's team gets back.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:00.115
Now watch the customer’s screen.

00:02:00.115 --> 00:02:04.960
With an ordinary reply, she waits and then sees the whole message.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.304
With streaming, words appear as they are produced.

00:02:08.304 --> 00:02:12.968
She gets feedback sooner, even if the same three calls still happen.

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:18.535
Removing an unnecessary call would change the amount of work itself.

00:02:18.535 --> 00:02:24.957
Latency is the time the customer waits, and in an agent it adds up across the steps.

00:02:24.957 --> 00:02:33.952
A model call takes time to read its input and produce its output. The length of the answer affects how long it takes to finish.

00:02:33.952 --> 00:02:41.816
Each tool has its own response time, and waiting for a busy carrier can account for much of a customer's delay.

00:02:41.816 --> 00:02:49.896
A customer waits through any steps that depend on one another. Measure each one to see where the time actually went.

00:02:49.896 --> 00:02:58.505
Streaming helps: the reply appears word by word as it is written, so the customer sees progress instead of a blank screen.

00:02:58.505 --> 00:03:05.800
Fewer steps, smaller models for quick steps, and streaming are your three main tools against waiting.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:11.746
Take the three example charges from Ana's order question and add them on paper.

00:03:11.746 --> 00:03:15.569
One point four plus one point six makes three cents.

00:03:15.569 --> 00:03:21.523
Add two point one and the full answer costs five point one cents in this example.

00:03:21.523 --> 00:03:25.621
Now picture the same three calls with streaming turned on.

00:03:25.621 --> 00:03:34.347
Ana can start reading earlier, but you have not removed a lookup or shortened the text simply by changing when it appears.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:37.966
Record both first word and completed answer.

00:03:37.966 --> 00:03:43.462
Try removing a genuinely repeated lookup and run the same question again.

00:03:43.462 --> 00:03:48.894
Compare cost, waiting time and whether the answer still matches the parcel.

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:55.378
Two responses are only useful to compare if they were supposed to do the same job.

00:03:55.378 --> 00:04:03.389
To find a slow or expensive change, record the time, usage, and errors for comparable order questions.

00:04:03.389 --> 00:04:05.700
Did the carrier take longer?

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.008
Did Scout repeat a lookup?

00:04:08.008 --> 00:04:11.597
Did accuracy change when a step was removed?

00:04:11.597 --> 00:04:14.355
Check the answer as well as the bill.

00:04:14.355 --> 00:04:19.914
A cheaper reply that gives the wrong parcel status is a poor trade.
